The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system operating in a multi-user environment with hierarchical roles, clearance levels, and support for temporary delegation and revocation of roles. Each user is assigned a security clearance (High, Medium, Low), and actions on system objects are controlled based on both clearance and classification levels. The system processes user action requests concurrently in FIFO order, ensuring authorization correctness, consistency, liveness, and safety. Administrators have special privileges and can delegate roles or revoke them. The Emergency Module introduces an Emergency mode, which is triggered by administrators, temporarily changes processing and privilege rules, and must revert the system to its normal state after a limited time.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
===========================================
- RBAC with role hierarchy and role assignment.
- Users are assigned a clearance level: High, Medium, Low.
- Administrators have High clearance, but not all High clearance users are Administrators.
- Actions (requests) have a classification level based on affected objects.
- Users may submit requests only if their clearance ≥ request classification.
- Request processing is concurrent but must be FIFO, and all requests are eventually processed.
- Higher clearance users may approve/reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Roles can be delegated temporarily from higher to lower clearance users, subject to minimum role clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or are revocable by administrators at any time.
- Authorization correctness, delegation/permission consistency, liveness, and safety under concurrency are required.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Mode Trigger
Exactly two administrators, while the system is in Normal mode, must act to trigger an Emergency, putting the system into Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration
Emergency mode is active for a limited time only. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after this time elapses.

R3: Emergency Mode Behaviors
    R3.1: Emergency Request Processing
    During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize multiple high classification requests for concurrent processing.
    
    R3.2: Mutual Exclusive Roles
    During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold roles that are normally mutually exclusive.
    
    R3.3: Emergency Administrator Privileges
    During Emergency mode, administrators hold additional emergency privileges.

R4: Emergency Mode Termination Actions
    R4.1: Emergency Privilege Revocation
    All emergency privileges granted during Emergency mode must be revoked once Emergency mode ends.
    
    R4.2: Delegated Role Revocation
    Any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit.
    
    R4.3: Administrator Credential Update
    Administrators must update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity with an assigned clearance level and possibly one or more roles (assigned or delegated).
- Administrator: A special user with High clearance and administrative privileges.
- Role: Permissions that can be assigned to or delegated among users, possibly with mutual exclusivity and clearance requirements.
- Clearance Level: {High, Medium, Low}, determines what actions/users can do/approve.
- Object: System resource with a classification level (High, Medium, Low).
- Request: User-submitted action affecting one or more objects; has a classification level.
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily granted from one user to another, with possible expiry/revocation.
- Emergency Mode: Special system state with relaxed or altered rules, limited in duration.
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges held by administrators during Emergency mode.
- Mutual Exclusive Roles: Roles that cannot be held by the same user under normal conditions.
- Emergency Credential Update: The process administrators must perform after Emergency mode ends.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. What constitutes “exactly two administrators” acting to trigger Emergency mode? (e.g., must the actions be simultaneous, within a window, or is an ordering allowed?)
2. What is the precise duration of Emergency mode? Is it fixed, configurable, or event-driven (e.g., manual override)?
3. Are emergency privileges for administrators well-defined, or should they be abstracted as a general set of temporary elevated permissions?
4. When administrators must update credentials after Emergency mode, is this a blocking transition to Normal mode, or can the system return to Normal mode before all credentials are updated?

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Emergency mode may only be entered from Normal mode and requires exactly two administrators to trigger.
- Emergency mode is time-bounded; system must guarantee eventual return to Normal mode.
- During Emergency mode, FIFO request processing may be bypassed for high classification requests.
- Users may temporarily violate mutual exclusion constraints on roles only during Emergency mode.
- Administrators obtain and must later relinquish emergency privileges strictly within Emergency mode boundaries.
- All emergency privileges and any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit.
- Administrator credential updates are mandatory after Emergency mode ends.
