**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system operating in a multi-user environment with hierarchical roles, clearance levels, and support for temporary delegation and revocation of roles. Each user is assigned a security clearance (High, Medium, Low), and actions on system objects are controlled based on both clearance and classification levels. The system processes user action requests concurrently in FIFO order, ensuring authorization correctness, consistency, liveness, and safety. Administrators have special privileges and can delegate roles or revoke them. The Emergency Module introduces an Emergency mode, which is triggered by two administrators concurrently, temporarily changes processing and privilege rules, and must revert the system to its normal state after a fixed duration or upon concurrent deactivation by two administrators. Only the administrators who trigger Emergency mode receive temporary emergency privileges. Upon returning to Normal mode, these administrators must update their credentials at the first time step.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Mode Trigger  
Exactly two administrators must act concurrently, while the system is in Normal mode, to trigger an Emergency, putting the system into Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active for a fixed, system-defined duration. However, Emergency mode may be manually ended early by the same two administrators acting concurrently to trigger Emergency deactivation. The system must return to Normal mode either upon expiration of the fixed duration or upon concurrent manual deactivation.

R3: Emergency Mode Behaviors  
    R3.1: Emergency Request Processing  
    During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize multiple high classification requests for concurrent processing.
    
    R3.2: Mutual Exclusive Roles  
    During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold roles that are normally mutually exclusive.
    
    R3.3: Emergency Administrator Privileges  
    During Emergency mode, only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode acquire a general set of temporary elevated emergency privileges, which are abstracted and not tied to specific administrator actions or permissions.

R4: Emergency Mode Termination Actions  
    R4.1: Emergency Privilege Revocation  
    All emergency privileges granted during Emergency mode must be revoked once Emergency mode ends.
    
    R4.2: Delegated Role Revocation  
    Any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit.
    
    R4.3: Administrator Credential Update  
    At the first time step after Emergency mode ends, the administrators who triggered Emergency mode must update their credentials.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity with an assigned clearance level and possibly one or more roles (assigned or delegated).
- Administrator: A special user with High clearance and administrative privileges.
- Role: Permissions that can be assigned to or delegated among users, possibly with mutual exclusivity and clearance requirements.
- Clearance Level: {High, Medium, Low}, determines what actions/users can do/approve.
- Object: System resource with a classification level (High, Medium, Low).
- Request: User-submitted action affecting one or more objects; has a classification level.
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily granted from one user to another, with possible expiry/revocation.
- Emergency Mode: Special system state with relaxed or altered rules, limited in duration and triggered by two administrators acting concurrently.
- Emergency Privileges: A general set of temporary elevated permissions granted only to the administrators who concurrently trigger Emergency mode, and held strictly within Emergency mode.
- Mutual Exclusive Roles: Roles that cannot be held by the same user under normal conditions.
- Emergency Credential Update: The process the triggering administrators must perform at the first time step after Emergency mode ends.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
None. All previously outstanding assumptions have been resolved per user clarifications.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Emergency mode may only be entered from Normal mode and requires exactly two administrators acting concurrently to trigger.
- Emergency mode is time-bounded with a fixed duration but may be ended early by concurrent action of the same two administrators.
- During Emergency mode, FIFO request processing may be bypassed for high classification requests.
- Users may temporarily violate mutual exclusion constraints on roles only during Emergency mode.
- Only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode receive emergency privileges, which must be relinquished strictly within Emergency mode boundaries.
- All emergency privileges and any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit from Emergency mode.
- Administrator credential updates by the triggering administrators are mandatory at the first time step after Emergency mode ends.
